latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Input Processing:\n   a. Accept the input ColoredGrid.\n   b. Determine the dimensions of the input grid.\n\n2. Background Color Identification:\n   a. Count the occurrences of each color in the grid.\n   b. Identify the most common color as the background color.\n\n3. Grid Quadrant Division:\n   a. Divide the input grid into four quadrants, regardless of whether the dimensions are even or odd.\n   b. Handle any remainder rows or columns by including them in the right and/or bottom quadrants.\n\n4. Color Rarity Calculation:\n   a. Calculate the frequency of each color in the entire grid.\n   b. Compute a rarity score for each color (1 - frequency).\n\n5. Shape Detection for Each Quadrant:\n   a. Implement a flood fill algorithm to identify connected regions of non-background colors.\n   b. For each region, store its color and list of coordinates.\n\n6. Shape Significance Scoring:\n   For each detected shape in a quadrant:\n   a. Calculate a size score (number of pixels / quadrant size).\n   b. Use the pre-calculated color rarity score.\n   c. Calculate a position score (proximity to the quadrant's corner).\n   d. Combine these scores with appropriate weights to get a final significance score.\n\n7. Shape Selection:\n   a. For each quadrant, select the shape with the highest significance score.\n\n8. Shape Compression:\n   For each selected shape:\n   a. Determine the bounding box of the shape.\n   b. Divide the bounding box into a 3x3 grid.\n   c. For each cell in the 3x3 grid, determine the most common color from the original shape in that area.\n\n9. Output Grid Creation:\n   a. Create a 7x7 grid filled with the background color.\n   b. Place each compressed 3x3 shape in its corresponding corner of the 7x7 grid.\n   c. Ensure the central cross (middle row and column) remains the background color.\n\n10. Result:\n    a. Return the 7x7 ColoredGrid as the output.\n\nImplementation Details:\n\n- Use the ColoredGrid class methods for grid manipulations where possible.\n- Implement helper functions for each major step (e.g., `identify_background()`, `divide_quadrants()`, `detect_shapes()`, `compress_shape()`, etc.).\n- Use efficient data structures like sets for tracking visited pixels in flood fill, and dictionaries for color counting and scoring.\n- Ensure all functions handle edge cases gracefully (e.g., empty quadrants, single-color quadrants).\n\nTesting and Refinement:\n\n- Implement the solution incrementally, testing each component with simple examples.\n- Use the provided test cases to verify the complete solution.\n- If any test cases fail, analyze the output to identify which step of the process needs refinement.\n- Iterate on the implementation, adjusting parameters (like scoring weights) or algorithms as needed to improve accuracy across all test cases.\n\nThis plan provides a structured approach to solving the challenge while addressing the key observations from our reflection. It allows for flexibility in handling various input sizes and shapes while aiming to capture the essence of the patterns in each quadrant."
